Add two cheap guards at the top of findSwapIndex, keeping both exported functions total for any input:

  function findSwapIndex(order, groupId, direction, hiddenGroups) {
    if (!Array.isArray(order) || !Array.isArray(hiddenGroups)) return -1;
    const from = order.indexOf(groupId);
    ...
  }

and make moveGroup tolerate a non-array order in its own copy step: `const next = Array.isArray(order) ? [...order] : [];`. With -1 returned, canMoveGroup already reports false and moveGroup already returns the order unchanged, so the existing agreement invariant is preserved for free. This also removes the string-hiddenGroups substring misbehaviour described above.

Mirror the existing sanitizer for the visibility set and apply it in the same unconditional block, e.g.

  export function sanitizeHiddenGroups(value: unknown): GroupId[] {
    if (!Array.isArray(value)) return [];
    return [...new Set(value.filter((id): id is GroupId => GROUP_IDS.includes(id as GroupId)))];
  }

called next to `board.groupOrder = sanitizeGroupOrder(board.groupOrder)` at src/data/migration.ts:153. This belongs to the migration/sanitization layer (Task 03 scope), not to Task 04 — file it against that layer rather than reopening this task. Keep the guard from finding 1 regardless: defence in depth is the point, and groupOrderUtils is unit-testable in isolation whereas the popup is not.
